<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.weather.dashboard_render</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>/Users/xiong/weather/scripts/dashboard_render_sweep.sh</string>
  </array>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Users/xiong/weather</string>
  <!-- Thirty minutes, which is the cadence the section cache was written
       against: its nine lifetimes run 150 to 270 minutes and are staggered
       so that no single render expires the whole set.

       The render used to be scheduled inside the resident, on its own
       executor, with a 2400s timeout attached.  That timeout was the reason
       the page went stale: a render that needs longer is SIGTERMed before it
       writes, and it writes nothing at all; measured 2026-09-09, a run
       that started 09:24:29Z was killed at 2408s having produced no file,
       and the page still carried a build from 07:52Z.  It is also self-
       reinforcing: a killed render refreshes no cached section, so the next
       one starts colder and is killed sooner in its own work.  The last
       render that completed reused nine of twenty-one sections and took
       678s; the ones being killed were computing all of them.

       A render is a reader.  It takes no write lock and nothing waits on
       it.  launchd imposes no timeout, and the script's own single_instance
       guard is what keeps a slow render from stacking: a start that arrives
       while one is running exits 0 having skipped.

       2026-09-23 (user ruling: 加超时): a reader can still starve every
       writer.  Throttled by taskpolicy -b behind a saturated disk, the
       08:07Z render sat five hours in uninterruptible I/O wait holding its
       read snapshot of the store; the WAL could not reset, grew from 64 MB
       to 14.9 GB, filled the disk to 285 MiB and the fleet produced nothing
       from 11:49Z.  The sweep script therefore carries its own deadline,
       PM_DASHBOARD_RENDER_TIMEOUT_SEC (default 1500 s: 419 renders took a
       median 10.8 min and at most 21.5 min on a healthy day, and five
       minutes under this interval leaves a gap with no render holding a
       snapshot).  The 2026-09-09 cost is accepted with eyes open: a killed
       render keeps the previous page, and a stale page is cheaper than a
       wedged store.  tests/test_dashboard_render_timeout.py runs the real
       script against a stub render. -->
  <!-- 2026-10-21 (user 「继续优化渲染周期」): wake every minute; the script
       starts a compute once 1,800 s have passed since the previous compute
       STARTED (data/dashboard_render.compute_started).  launchd counts its
       interval from the previous exit, which behind a 20-25 minute compute
       had made the real cycle 50-55 minutes. -->
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>60</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <!-- The kill left no trace anywhere on 2026-09-09: the resident recorded
       timeout_killed in a status field nothing on the page reads, and the
       render itself had no log of its own. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/xiong/weather/data/logs/dashboard_render.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/xiong/weather/data/logs/dashboard_render.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
